Section 5. Cause Analysis
The evidence identifies several plausible contributors. These are working explanations rather than confirmed causes.
5.1 Unclear Performance Expectations
The client and teachers report a lack of explicit course and level-specific outcomes. This may make it difficult to determine what learners should demonstrate independently and when additional support is required.
5.2 Limited Transfer Opportunities
Teachers describe limited writing practice and greater difficulty with mixed grammar tasks. Students may receive insufficient opportunities to move from practicing an identified structure to choosing and applying structures independently.
5.3 Insufficient Support for Written Production
Assignment findings and teacher observations suggest difficulty moving from prompts or examples to complete written responses.
Support may be needed for sentence construction, prompt interpretation, idea selection, and organization. These needs should be distinguished rather than treated as a single grammar problem.
5.4 Feedback and Follow-Up
Some reviewed corrections are difficult to distinguish or lack detail. Teachers also report that checking whether students can apply corrections in a new task is not consistently built into instruction.
This may limit opportunities to identify persistent misunderstandings. However, visible markings alone do not establish the absence of oral feedback.
5.5 Variation in Prerequisite Skills
Vocabulary, reading comprehension, sentence construction, and independence vary among learners. A student’s difficulty may arise from misunderstanding the prompt, lacking an expression, or applying a grammar rule incorrectly.
Further diagnosis should distinguish these possibilities before selecting instructional responses.
5.6 Explanatory Support and Learner–Task Fit
The unit-level review raises a concern about whether grammar explanations and demonstrations sufficiently support understanding and independent application.
In this project, greater instructional depth should mean clearer relationships, contextualized examples, contrasting errors, and guided application. It does not necessarily require more advanced grammar content or extensive grammatical terminology.
Writing tasks may also require several skills simultaneously: prompt comprehension, vocabulary retrieval, sentence construction, spelling, and organization. Where these prerequisite skills are not yet stable, limited scaffolding may make independent writing difficult.
Teacher reports of dependence on prompts and assignment findings involving incomplete sentences are consistent with this explanation. However, they do not establish that brief textbook explanations caused the observed difficulties. Prior knowledge, actual classroom instruction, practice opportunities, and feedback remain relevant influences.
