Section 6. Instructional and Non-Instructional Factors
6.1 Is Instruction Part of the Solution?
Instruction is a justified part of the response because the available evidence shows difficulties in grammar application and written production.
Supplementary instruction could address:
* Explicit explanation of selected grammar relationships.
* Contextualized and mixed practice.
* Sentence construction.
* Guided progression toward independent writing.
* Feedback followed by correction and reapplication.
* Review of previously taught content.
The evidence does not establish that instruction alone will resolve the problem.
6.2 Non-Instructional Factors
Relevant contextual and organizational factors include:
* Heavy school homework demands.
* Limited completion of optional supplementary work.
* Parents’ requests to reduce additional homework.
* Limited parental capacity to support English learning.
* Restricted classroom time.
* Teacher turnover and incomplete preparation.
* Unconfirmed technology budget and teacher availability.
* Management approval requirements.
The proposed product would also be optional to purchase. Access, participation, and completion may therefore differ among students. These differences must be considered when interpreting later evaluation results.
6.3 Learning and Performance Contexts
The proposed learning context is self-paced, supplementary study outside scheduled lessons. Grammar work may be self-checked, while writing may receive teacher feedback.
The intended performance contexts include independent textbook-related tasks, writing activities, and examinations. Examination conditions and scoring requirements have not yet been documented sufficiently to define a single assessment environment.
Supplementary learning should therefore progressively reduce support and include independent application tasks, while preserving assistance during learning.
6.4 Implementation Feasibility
The client has expressed willingness to provide two teachers and arrange small-group formative evaluation, subject to management approval and necessary parental permission.
Before implementation, the project needs confirmation of:
* Initial development scope and schedule.
* Teacher responsibilities and available time.
* Technology costs.
* Student access and usability.
* Feedback procedures.
* Evaluation arrangements.
Notion access permissions should not be described as guaranteed copyright protection or as preventing all sharing. Platform capabilities and practical access controls require verification.
